TI  - HECTD1 Directs HIF-1α to Facilitate Intestinal Inflammation and Carcinogenesis 
      via Promoting Th17 Differentiation and Glycolysis.
PG  - e78339
LID - 10.1002/advs.78339 [doi]
AB  - Targeting glucose metabolism in Th17 cells may be beneficial for treating 
      inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) and colitis-associated cancer (CAC), however, 
      the molecular mechanisms orchestrating Th17 cell metabolism remain unclear. Here, 
      we revealed that HECTD1 regulates IGF2BP3-dependent HIF-1α translation to promote 
      Th17 cell glycolysis and differentiation and that HECTD1 expression correlates 
      with IBD and CAC pathogenesis. T-cell-specific deletion of HECTD1 mitigated the 
      severity of colitis and CAC in murine models. RNA-seq revealed enrichment of Th17 
      differentiation, glycolysis/gluconeogenesis, and HIF-1 signaling pathways among 
      differentially expressed genes between HECTD1-sufficient and HECTD1-deficient 
      CD4(+) T cells. Consistently, loss of HECTD1 suppressed Th17 cell differentiation 
      both in vitro and in vivo, and suppressed glycolytic metabolism and HIF-1α 
      activity, revealing the mechanism underlying its protective effects against IBD 
      and CAC. Mechanistically, HECTD1 potentiates m(6)A-based HIF-1α translation by 
      mediating IGF2BP3 K63-linked ubiquitination to promote the binding of IGF2BP3 
      with the translation initiation machinery of Hif1a mRNA. Reciprocally, HIF-1α 
      acts as a key transcription factor that promotes Hectd1 gene expression, 
      establishing a distinct positive feedback loop. Our findings highlight a 
      self-reinforcing feedback mechanism in which HECTD1 and HIF-1α engage in mutual 
      regulation to dictate Th17 cell fate and metabolic reprogramming, providing novel 
      potential IBD and CAC treatments targeting HECTD1.
