TI  - Role of HIF1α in FXR-mediated protection and anti-inflammatory macrophage 
      polarization in parenteral nutrition-associated cholestasis.
PG  - 168410
LID - S0925-4439(26)00273-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bbadis.2026.168410 [doi]
AB  - Parenteral nutrition-associated cholestasis (PNAC) arises from suppressed FXR 
      signaling and macrophage activation driven by intestinal LPS and PN-derived 
      phytosterols. We investigated whether tissue hypoxia and HIF1α signaling play a 
      role in FXR-mediated protection in PNAC. PNAC mouse intrahepatic mononuclear 
      cells (IHMCs) displayed evidence of hypoxia and upregulation of HIF1α and 
      hypoxia-responsive genes, also demonstrated in livers from human infants with 
      PNAC. Myeloid-specific Hif1a knock-out reduced liver injury, decreased macrophage 
      infiltration, restored FXR signaling, and promoted anti-inflammatory gene 
      expression in PNAC mice. HIF1α physically interacted with the histone 
      methyltransferase enzyme, EZH2 in PNAC liver, an effect disrupted by FXR agonist 
      GW4064, which modified EZH2 phosphorylation by activating AMPK/AKT kinases, 
      diminishing H3K27me3 repression at anti-inflammatory loci. Furthermore, GW4064 
      reduced pro-inflammatory gene expression while inducing anti-inflammatory genes 
      in IHMCs, decreased hepatocyte apoptosis, and enhanced HIF1α/H19 interaction. 
      Inhibition of EZH2 with Tazemetostat reduced liver injury by decreasing 
      pro-inflammatory markers. In conclusion, HIF1α-EZH2 signaling promoted hepatic 
      inflammation and injury in PNAC, and FXR activation counteracted this axis to 
      promote anti-inflammatory macrophage polarization.
