TI  - Gut microbiota-derived propionate mediates the protective effects of green banana 
      resistant starch on ulcerative colitis by inhibiting HDAC1 to promote HIF-1α 
      acetylation.
PG  - 120231
LID - S0963-9969(26)01918-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.foodres.2026.120231 [doi]
AB  - Ulcerative colitis (UC) is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease with increasing 
      global incidence, lacking specific targeted drugs. Green banana resistant starch 
      (RS) has been shown to possess antioxidant effects and alleviate gastric ulcers 
      and diarrhea, suggesting its potential therapeutic value for UC. However, its 
      specific efficacy and mechanism of action remain unclear. In a dextran sulfate 
      sodium-induced mouse model of UC, this study demonstrated that RS attenuates 
      inflammatory responses and oxidative stress, preserves intestinal barrier 
      function, and enriches beneficial commensals including Muribaculaceae, with a 
      concomitant increase in propionate (PA) production. Its protective effects were 
      microbiota-dependent, as shown in pseudo-germ-free mice. PA activated GPR43, 
      triggering the SP1 pathway to suppress HDAC1 expression. Reduced HDAC1 increased 
      HIF-1α acetylation, upregulating tight junction proteins (ZO-1, Occludin) and 
      MUC2 while inhibiting NFκB/NLRP3 signaling and improving oxidative markers. PA 
      supplementation mimicked RS benefits, whereas an HDAC activator counteracted 
      them, thereby highlighting the central role of the HDAC1/HIF-1α axis. These 
      results establish the mechanistic basis by which RS-derived propionate targets 
      the HDAC1/HIF-1α axis against colitis, highlighting the potential of RS as a gut 
      microbiota-targeting nutraceutical for UC management.
