TI  - Mosaic EPAS1 Gain-of-Function Spectrum.
BTI - GeneReviews(®)
AB  - CLINICAL CHARACTERISTICS: The phenotypic spectrum due to mosaic EPAS1 
      gain-of-function pathogenic variants has traditionally been divided into 
      Pacak-Zhuang syndrome (PZS; classically defined as polycythemia with 
      paraganglioma or somatostatinoma associated with other syndromic features) and 
      apparently isolated pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma (PPGL) without other systemic 
      features. PZS often manifests early in life as postnatal polycythemia, very low 
      ferritin levels, vascular malformations (including dilated capillaries, tortuous 
      retinal vessels, and dilated venous plexus in the central nervous system), and 
      tumors of chromaffin cells such as paragangliomas and pheochromocytomas (also 
      known as intra-adrenal paragangliomas). Less common features may include 
      vertebral segmentation anomalies, other eye anomalies (morning glory anomaly, 
      foveal hypoplasia/aplasia), marfanoid habitus, scoliosis, spinal dysraphism, and 
      Chari I malformation. Individuals with EPAS1-related isolated PPGL do not have 
      polycythemia or other syndromic features. DIAGNOSIS/TESTING: The diagnosis of PZS 
      or EPAS1-related isolated PPGL is established in a proband with suggestive 
      findings of each condition, respectively, AND a heterozygous mosaic activating 
      pathogenic variant in EPAS1 identified by molecular genetic testing. MANAGEMENT: 
      Targeted therapy: Hypoxia-inducible factor 2-alpha (HIF2α) inhibitor (e.g., 
      belzutifan) may be used for the treatment of tumor burden related to 
      pheochromocytoma or paragangliomas, particularly in those who have undergone 
      multiple surgical resections or in individuals who are not amenable to surgery 
      (multifocal tumors, metastatic disease, or location that would jeopardize vital 
      organs). Treatment may also lead to normalization of catecholamines and related 
      effects (hypertension, tachyarrhythmias) and erythropoietin level (which may 
      obviate the need for phlebotomy). Belzutifan may be considered in the treatment 
      of vascular eye lesions when vision is threatened. Supportive care: Treatment of 
      catecholamine-induced hypertension and tachyarrhythmia may also respond to 
      alpha-adrenoceptor blockade and (in select cases with tachyarrhythmia) 
      beta-adrenoceptor blockade. Vascular eye lesions or macular edema may respond to 
      intravitreal vascular endothelial growth factor inhibitors or focal laser therapy 
      targeted to the affected areas. Standard treatment for Chiari I malformation, 
      spinal dysraphism, and scoliosis. Surveillance: Obtain hemoglobin, hematocrit, 
      iron, and ferritin levels every three to four months in those with polycythemia 
      who are treated with venesection (phlebotomy). Annual clinical assessment for 
      manifestations of PPGL in conjunction with obtaining plasma-free fractionated 
      metanephrines or 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines (optional dopamine or 
      3-methoxytyramine) for secreting PPGL. MRI (head and neck; thoracic, abdomen, and 
      pelvis) for PPGL every one to two years with consideration of (18)F-fluorodopa 
      (FDOPA) PET/CT per treating clinicians. Measure somatostatin level and liver 
      enzymes (aspartate transaminase, alanine transaminase, alkaline phosphatase, and 
      gamma-glutamyl transferase) and obtain a negative enteric contrast CT or 
      endoscopy, as clinically indicated, to evaluate for somatostatinoma. 
      Ophthalmology evaluation, including dilated fundoscopic examination and 
      fluorescein angiography, annually or as clinically indicated. Assess for signs 
      and symptoms of Chiari I malformation at each visit. Screening for metastatic 
      disease in individuals who underwent surgical resection for PPGL should include 
      annual assessment of plasma-free fractionated metanephrines or 24-hour urine 
      fractionated metanephrines (optional dopamine or 3-methoxytyramine) to screen for 
      secreting PPGL and annual CT or MRI with contrast of the neck, chest, abdomen, 
      and pelvis or PET scan ((68)Ga-DOTATATE or preferably (18)F-FDOPA when available) 
      to assess for localization of smaller tumors. Agents/circumstances to avoid: 
      Exposure to higher altitudes and other conditions that induce hypoxia, including 
      activities that result in significant tissue hypoxia, such as strenuous exercise. 
      GENETIC COUNSELING: PZS and mosaic EPAS1-related isolated PPGL are not known to 
      be inherited. Most individuals diagnosed with PZS or mosaic EPAS1-related 
      isolated PPGL have a postzygotic (and thus mosaic) EPAS1 pathogenic variant. No 
      confirmed vertical transmission or sib recurrence has been reported to date.
