TI  - Molecular mechanisms of dragon's blood in treating ulcerative colitis based on 
      NF-κb/NLPR3/Caspase-1 pyroptosis signaling pathway.
PG  - e0331570
LID - 10.1371/journal.pone.0331570 [doi]
LID - e0331570
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To elucidate the molecular mechanism of dragon's blood (DB) in the 
      treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC). METHODS: Bioactive metabolites of DB 
      absorbed into the bloodstream were characterized via LC-MS. Network pharmacology 
      and molecular docking were employed to construct a target-pathway interaction 
      model predicting DB's therapeutic mechanism in UC. A 4% DSS-induced UC mouse 
      model was used for experimental validation. RESULTS: DB markedly alleviated 
      colonic injury in DSS-induced UC. A total of 255 active compounds were 
      identified, including 6,4'-Dimethoxy-7-hydroxyisoflavone, 
      7-hydroxy-2-(4-methoxyphenyl) chromen-4-one, and Apigenin. Key molecular targets 
      included NLPR3, MAPK1, TP53, HIF1A, and PTGS2. The NF-κB/NLPR3/Caspase-1 axis was 
      implicated as a central pathway mediating the therapeutic effects of DB. 
      CONCLUSION: DB acts through a multi-component, multi-target, and multi-pathway 
      strategy. Inhibiting the NF-κB/NLPR3/Caspase-1 pyroptosis pathway positions NLPR3 
      as a viable target for DB in UC intervention.
