TI  - Metabolic disruption exacerbates intestinal damage during sleep deprivation by 
      abolishing HIF1α-mediated repair.
PG  - 114915
LID - S2211-1247(24)01266-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.celrep.2024.114915 [doi]
AB  - Sleep deprivation (SD) has been reported to induce intestinal damage by several 
      mechanisms, yet its role in modulating epithelial repair remains unclear. In this 
      study, we find that chronic SD leads to colonic damage through continuous 
      hypoxia. However, HIF1α, which generally responds to hypoxia to modulate barrier 
      integrity, was paradoxically dysregulated in the colon. Further investigation 
      revealed that a metabolic disruption during SD causes accumulation of 
      α-ketoglutarate in the colon. The excessive α-ketoglutarate degrades HIF1α 
      protein through PHD2 (prolyl hydroxylase 2) to abolish the intestinal repair 
      functions of HIF1α. Collectively, these findings provide insights into how SD can 
      exacerbate intestinal damage by fine-tuning metabolism to abolish HIF1α-mediated 
      repair.
