VI  - 591
DP  - 2024 Jun 1
TI  - Tumor-associated macrophage-induced circMRCKα encodes a peptide to promote 
      glycolysis and progression in hepatocellular carcinoma.
PG  - 216872
LID - S0304-3835(24)00265-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.canlet.2024.216872 [doi]
AB  - The tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) play multifaceted roles in the 
      progression of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). However, the involvement of 
      circular RNAs in the interplay between TAMs and HCC remains unclear. Based on 
      Transwell co-culturing and circular RNA sequencing, this study revealed that TAMs 
      enhanced tumor glycolysis and progression by upregulating circMRCKα in HCC cells. 
      Patients with HCC who exhibited elevated circMRCKα levels presented significantly 
      reduced overall survival and greater cumulative recurrence. Notably, we 
      identified a novel functional peptide of 227 amino acids named circMRCKα-227aa, 
      encoded by circMRCKα. Mechanistically, circMRCKα-227aa bound to USP22 and 
      enhanced its protein level to obstruct HIF-1α degradation via the 
      ubiquitin-proteasome pathway, thereby augmenting HCC glycolysis and progression. 
      In clinical HCC samples, a positive correlation was observed between the 
      expression of circMRCKα and the number of infiltrating CD68(+) TAMs and 
      expression of USP22. Furthermore, circMRCKα emerged as an independent prognostic 
      risk factor both individually and in conjunction with CD68(+) TAMs and USP22. 
      This study illustrated that circMRCKα-227aa, a novel TAM-induced peptide, 
      promotes tumor glycolysis and progression via USP22 binding and HIF-1α 
      upregulation, suggesting that circMRCKα and TAMs could be combined as therapeutic 
      targets in HCC.
