MH  - Cell Proliferation/genetics
OAB - A primary goal in the treatment of cancer is preventing the formation of new 
      blood vessels, or angiogenesis, within the tumor, because these newly formed 
      capillaries serve to supply tumor cells with oxygen, letting them live for longer 
      periods of time and develop several other unfavorable traits that would 
      complicate the entire treatment process. Although certain molecules are 
      responsible for regulating angiogenesis, others such as lncRNA H19, cause 
      significant deregulation in the level of these antiangiogenic molecules, 
      enhancing tumor vascularization. Because H19 is induced in response to cigarette 
      smoke, individuals who smoke might be at higher risk of treatment failure as a 
      result of accelerated angiogenesis.
