TI  - Design and synthesis of 14 and 15-membered macrocyclic scaffolds exhibiting 
      inhibitory activities of hypoxia-inducible factor 1α.
PG  - 115949
LID - S0968-0896(20)30779-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bmc.2020.115949 [doi]
AB  - Inspired by the privileged molecular skeletons of 14- and 15-membered 
      antibiotics, we adopted a relatively unexplored synthetic approach that exploits 
      alkaloidal macrocyclic scaffolds to generate modulators of protein-protein 
      interactions (PPIs). As mimetics of hot-spot residues in the α-helices 
      responsible for the transcriptional regulation, three hydrophobic sidechains were 
      displayed on each of the four distinct macrocyclic scaffolds generating diversity 
      of their spatial arrangements. Modular assembly of the building blocks followed 
      by ring-closing olefin metathesis reaction and subsequent hydrogenation allowed 
      concise and divergent synthesis of scaffolds 1-4. The 14-membered alkaloidal 
      macrocycles 2-4 demonstrated similar inhibition of hypoxia-inducible factor 
      (HIF)-1α transcriptional activities (IC(50) between 8.7 and 10 µM), and 4 
      demonstrated the most potent inhibition of cell proliferation in vitro 
      (IC(50) = 12 µM against HTC116 colon cancer cell line). A docking model suggested 
      that 4 could mimic the LLxxL motif in HIF-1α, in which the three sidechains are 
      capable of matching the spatial arrangements of the protein hot-spot residues. 
      Unlike most of the stapled peptides, the 14-membered alkaloidal scaffold has a 
      similar size to the α-helix backbone and does not require additional atoms to 
      induce α-helix mimetic structure. These experimental results underscore the 
      potential of alkaloidal macrocyclic scaffolds featuring flexibly customizable 
      skeletal, stereochemical, substitutional, and conformational properties for the 
      development of non-peptidyl PPI modulators targeting α-helix-forming consensus 
      sequences responsible for the transcriptional regulation.
