TI  - At the crossroads of oxygen and iron sensing: hepcidin control of HIF-2α.
PG  - 72-74
LID - 125509 [pii]
LID - 10.1172/JCI125509 [doi]
AB  - Hepcidin is the master regulator of iron metabolism. It plays a key role in the 
      regulation of iron transport across the duodenal epithelium, which in turn is 
      dependent on the oxygen-regulated transcription factor hypoxia-inducible factor 
      2α (HIF-2α). In this issue of the JCI, Schwartz and colleagues show that duodenal 
      HIF-2α is itself regulated by hepcidin, thereby indicating that this 
      transcription factor is not only regulated by oxygen, but also by iron. This work 
      indicates that the crosstalk between liver hepcidin and intestinal HIF-2α plays 
      an important role during iron overload, systemic iron deficiency, and anemia.
