TI  - Pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase 1 and carbonic anhydrase IX targeting in hypoxic 
      tumors.
PG  - 63-72
LID - 180531N357 [pii]
LID - 10.4149/neo_2018_180531N357 [doi]
AB  - Pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase 1 (PDHK1) and carbonic anhydrase IX (CAIX) are some 
      of the most hypoxia-inducible proteins associated with tumors, implicated in 
      glucose metabolism and pH regulation, respectively. They both appear to be 
      necessary for model tumor growth, and their high level of expression in human 
      tumors predicts poor patient outcome. Another thing they have in common is that 
      hypoxia not only induces their expression but also their enzymatic activity. This 
      work therefore simultaneously targets these two hypoxia-inducible proteins either 
      pharmacologically or genetically in vitro and in vivo, leading to decreased 
      cancer cell survival and significantly slower model tumor growth. It also 
      suggests that CAIX and PDHK1 are important for cells originating from a 
      colorectal primary tumor, as well as from its metastasis. Moreover, our analysis 
      reveals a unique relationship between these two HIF-1 target genes. In 
      conclusion, the attributes of PDHK1 and CAIX predict them to be promising targets 
      for the design of new, specific inhibitors that could negatively influence tumor 
      cell proliferation and survival, or increase efficacy of standard treatment 
      regimens, and at the same time avoid normal tissue toxicity.
