DP  - 2017 Feb 21
TI  - In vivo genome editing with a small Cas9 orthologue derived from Campylobacter 
      jejuni.
PG  - 14500
LID - 10.1038/ncomms14500 [doi]
LID - 14500
AB  - Several CRISPR-Cas9 orthologues have been used for genome editing. Here, we 
      present the smallest Cas9 orthologue characterized to date, derived from 
      Campylobacter jejuni (CjCas9), for efficient genome editing in vivo. After 
      determining protospacer-adjacent motif (PAM) sequences and optimizing 
      single-guide RNA (sgRNA) length, we package the CjCas9 gene, its sgRNA sequence, 
      and a marker gene in an all-in-one adeno-associated virus (AAV) vector and 
      produce the resulting virus at a high titer. CjCas9 is highly specific, cleaving 
      only a limited number of sites in the human or mouse genome. CjCas9, delivered 
      via AAV, induces targeted mutations at high frequencies in mouse muscle cells or 
      retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) cells. Furthermore, CjCas9 targeted to the Vegfa 
      or Hif1a gene in RPE cells reduces the size of laser-induced choroidal 
      neovascularization, suggesting that in vivo genome editing with CjCas9 is a new 
      option for the treatment of age-related macular degeneration.
