DP  - 2016 Mar 8
TI  - HIF-3α1 promotes colorectal tumor cell growth by activation of JAK-STAT3 
      signaling.
PG  - 11567-79
LID - 10.18632/oncotarget.7272 [doi]
AB  - Hypoxic environment is critical in colorectal cancer (CRC) development. Most 
      studies have mainly focused on hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)-1α and HIF-2α as 
      the major hypoxic transcription factors in CRC development and progression. 
      However, the role of HIF-3α in CRC is not clear. Here we found that HIF-3α 
      protein was increased in colorectal tumors from both mouse models and human 
      patients. Moreover, increased HIF-3α expression was correlated with decreased 
      survival. Overexpression of a long isoform of HIF-3α, HIF-3α1, increased cell 
      growth in two CRC cell lines. Surprisingly, overexpressed HIF-3α1 was localized 
      to the cytosol and increased phosphorylated signal transducer and activator of 
      transcription 3 (p-STAT3). STAT3 inhibition effectively reduced p-STAT3 levels 
      and cell growth induced by HIF-3α1. The activation of p-STAT3 was independent of 
      the transcriptional activity of HIF-3α1. However, the inhibition of the upstream 
      regulator Janus kinase (JAK) abolished HIF-3α1-induced p-STAT3 and cell growth. 
      Together, these results demonstrated that HIF-3α1 promotes CRC cell growth by 
      activation of the JAK-STAT3 signaling pathway through non-canonical 
      transcription-independent mechanisms.
