DP  - 2015 Nov 19
TI  - Salternamide A Suppresses Hypoxia-Induced Accumulation of HIF-1α and Induces 
      Apoptosis in Human Colorectal Cancer Cells.
PG  - 6962-76
LID - 10.3390/md13116962 [doi]
AB  - Hypoxia inducible factor-1α (HIF-1α) is an essential regulator of the cellular 
      response to low oxygen concentrations, activating a broad range of genes that 
      provide adaptive responses to oxygen deprivation. HIF-1α is overexpressed in 
      various cancers and therefore represents a considerable chemotherapeutic target. 
      Salternamide A (SA), a novel small molecule that is isolated from a halophilic 
      Streptomyces sp., is a potent cytotoxic agent against a variety of human cancer 
      cell lines. However, the mechanisms by which SA inhibits tumor growth remain to 
      be elucidated. In the present study, we demonstrate that SA efficiently inhibits 
      the hypoxia-induced accumulation of HIF-1α in a time- and concentration-dependent 
      manner in various human cancer cells. In addition, SA suppresses the upstream 
      signaling of HIF-1α, such as PI3K/Akt/mTOR, p42/p44 MAPK, and STAT3 signaling 
      under hypoxic conditions. Furthermore, we found that SA induces cell death by 
      stimulating G2/M cell cycle arrest and apoptosis in human colorectal cancer 
      cells. Taken together, SA was identified as a novel small molecule HIF-1α 
      inhibitor from marine natural products and is potentially a leading candidate in 
      the development of anticancer agents.
