TI  - Therapeutic inhibition of prolyl hydroxylase domain-containing enzymes in 
      surgery: putative applications and challenges.
PG  - 1-14
AB  - Oxygen is essential for metazoans to generate energy. Upon oxygen deprivation 
      adaptive and protective pathways are induced, mediated by hypoxia-inducible 
      factors (HIFs) and prolyl hydroxylase domain-containing enzymes (PHDs). Both play 
      a pivotal role in various conditions associated with prolonged ischemia and 
      inflammation, and are promising targets for therapeutic intervention. This review 
      focuses on aspects of therapeutic PHD modulation in surgically relevant disease 
      conditions such as hepatic and intestinal disorders, wound healing, innate immune 
      responses, and tumorigenesis, and discusses the therapeutic potential and 
      challenges of PHD inhibition in surgical patients.
