TI  - HIG2 promotes colorectal cancer progression via hypoxia-dependent and independent 
      pathways.
PG  - 159-65
LID - S0304-3835(13)00548-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.canlet.2013.07.028 [doi]
AB  - HIG2 (hypoxia-inducible gene 2) is a biomarker of hypoxia and elevated in several 
      cancers. Here, we show that HIG2 also upregulated HIF-1α expression under hypoxic 
      conditions and enhanced AP-1 expression under normoxic conditions, which affects 
      colorectal cancer cell survival. Importantly, over-expression of HIG2 promoted 
      tumor growth by suppressing apoptosis in a mouse orthotopic model. These results 
      are likely relevant to human disease since we found that the expression of HIG2 
      is gradually elevated as tumors progress. Collectively, these findings suggest 
      that HIG2 plays an important role in promoting colorectal cancer growth in 
      hypoxia-dependent and independent manners.
