DP  - 2012 Oct 11
TI  - Angiogenesis in metastatic colorectal cancer and the benefits of targeted 
      therapy.
PG  - 63
LID - 10.1186/1756-8722-5-63 [doi]
AB  - The diverse pathways and molecules involved in angiogenesis, the formation of new 
      blood vessels, have been targeted for the treatment of colorectal and other 
      cancers. Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)-A binding to VEGF receptor 
      (VEGFR)-2 is believed to be the key signaling pathway mediating angiogenesis. 
      Other VEGF pathways involved in angiogenesis include VEGF-A, VEGF-B, and 
      placental growth factor binding to VEGFR-1, and VEGF-C and VEGF-D binding to 
      VEGFR-2 and VEGFR-3. VEGF signaling also intersects with other pathways, 
      including angiopoietin/Tie, Notch, hypoxia-inducible factor, and integrin 
      pathways. The roles of these pathways in tumor angiogenesis and in various human 
      cancers will be explored in this article. In addition, preclinical and clinical 
      data on bevacizumab, aflibercept (known as ziv-aflibercept in the US), and 
      investigational antiangiogenic agents in development for the treatment of 
      colorectal and other cancers will be reviewed.
