TI  - Angiogenesis-related gene expression analysis in celiac disease.
PG  - 264-70
LID - 10.3109/08916934.2011.637531 [doi]
AB  - Celiac disease (CD) involves disturbance of the small-bowel mucosal vascular 
      network, and transglutaminase autoantibodies (TGA) have been related to 
      angiogenesis disturbance, a complex phenomenon probably also influenced by common 
      genetic variants in angiogenesis-related genes. A set of genes with 
      "angiogenesis" GO term identified in a previous expression microarray experiment 
      (SCG2, STAB1, TGFA, ANG, ERBB2, GNA13, PML, CASP8, ECGF1, JAG1, HIF1A, TNFSF13 
      and TGM2) was selected for genetic and functional studies. SNPs that showed a 
      trend for association with CD in the first GWAS were genotyped in 555 patients 
      and 541 controls. Gene expression of all genes was quantified in 15 pairs of 
      intestinal biopsies (diagnosis vs. GFD) and in three-dimensional HUVEC and T84 
      cell cultures incubated with TGA-positive and negative serum. A regulatory SNP in 
      TNFSF13 (rs11552708) is associated with CD (p = 0.01, OR = 0.7). Expression 
      changes in biopsies pointed to TGM2 and PML as up-regulated antiangiogenic genes 
      and to GNA13, TGFA, ERBB2 and SCG2 as down-regulated proangiogenic factors in CD. 
      TGA seem to enhance TGM2 expression in both cell models, but PML expression was 
      induced only in T84 enterocytes while GNA13 and ERBB2 were repressed in HUVEC 
      endothelial cells, with several genes showing discordant effects in each model, 
      highlighting the complexity of gene interactions in the pathogenesis of CD. 
      Finally, cell culture models are useful tools to help dissect complex responses 
      observed in human explants.
