DP  - 2011 Sep 27
TI  - A protein therapeutic modality founded on molecular regulation.
PG  - 16206-11
LID - 10.1073/pnas.1102803108 [doi]
AB  - The exquisite specificity of proteins is a key feature driving their application 
      to anticancer therapies. The therapeutic potential of another fundamental 
      property of proteins, their ability to be regulated by molecular cues in their 
      environment, is unknown. Here, we describe a synthetic biology strategy for 
      designing protein therapeutics that autonomously activate a therapeutic function 
      in response to a specific cancer marker of choice. We demonstrate this approach 
      by creating a prodrug-activating enzyme that selectively kills human cancer cells 
      that accumulate the marker hypoxia-inducible factor 1α. This property arises 
      primarily through increased cellular accumulation of the enzyme in the presence 
      of the marker. Our strategy offers a platform for the development of inherently 
      selective protein therapeutics for cancer and other diseases.
