DP  - 2011 Nov
TI  - Role of cyclophilin B in tumorigenesis and cisplatin resistance in hepatocellular 
      carcinoma in humans.
PG  - 1661-78
LID - 10.1002/hep.24539 [doi]
AB  - Cyclophilin B (CypB) performs diverse roles in living cells, but its role in 
      hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is largely unclear. To reveal its role in HCC, we 
      investigated the induction of CypB under hypoxia and its functions in tumor cells 
      in vitro and in vivo. Here, we demonstrated that hypoxia-inducible factor 1α 
      (HIF-1α) induces CypB under hypoxia. Interestingly, CypB protected tumor cells, 
      even p53-defective HCC cells, against hypoxia- and cisplatin-induced apoptosis. 
      Furthermore, it regulated the effects of HIF-1α, including those in angiogenesis 
      and glucose metabolism, via a positive feedback loop with HIF-1α. The tumorigenic 
      and chemoresistant effects of CypB were confirmed in vivo using a xenograft 
      model. Finally, we showed that CypB is overexpressed in 78% and 91% of the human 
      HCC and colon cancer tissues, respectively, and its overexpression in these 
      cancers reduced patient survival. CONCLUSIONS: These results indicate that CypB 
      induced by hypoxia stimulates the survival of HCC via a positive feedback loop 
      with HIF-1α, indicating that CypB is a novel candidate target for developing 
      chemotherapeutic agents against HCC and colon cancer.
