VI  - 9
IP  - 2
DP  - 2009 Feb
TI  - Intestinal hypoxia-inducible transcription factors are essential for iron 
      absorption following iron deficiency.
PG  - 152-64
LID - 10.1016/j.cmet.2008.12.012 [doi]
AB  - Iron deficiency and iron overload are among the most prevalent nutritional 
      disorders worldwide. Duodenal cytochrome b (DcytB) and divalent metal transporter 
      1 (DMT1) are regulators of iron absorption. Their expression is increased during 
      high systemic requirements for iron, but the molecular mechanisms that regulate 
      DcytB and DMT1 expression are undefined. Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) signaling 
      was induced in the intestine following acute iron deficiency in the duodenum, 
      resulting in activation of DcytB and DMT1 expression and an increase in iron 
      uptake. DcytB and DMT1 were demonstrated as direct HIF-2alpha target genes. 
      Genetic disruption of HIF signaling in the intestine abolished the adaptive 
      induction of iron absorption following iron deficiency, resulting in low systemic 
      iron and hematological defects. These results demonstrate that HIF signaling in 
      the intestine is a critical regulator of systemic iron homeostasis.
