DP  - 2006 Aug 7
TI  - Expression of NOS and HIF-1alpha in human colorectal carcinoma and implication in 
      tumor angiogenesis.
PG  - 4660-4
AB  - AIM: To study CD34, CD105, inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS), endogenous 
      nitric oxide synthase (eNOS), and hypoxia-inducible factor 1 (HIF-1) alpha 
      expression in human colorectal carcinomas. METHODS: The tissue microarrays (TMAs) 
      were made up of 80 cases of colorectal carcinoma and 80 cases of non-neoplasm 
      colorectal mucosa. The expression of CD34, CD105, NOS and HIF-1alpha was detected 
      by immunohistochemistry (S-P). RESULTS: iNOS and HIF-1alpha expression in 
      colorectal carcinoma was significantly higher than in non-neoplasm colorectal 
      mucosa (c2 = 43.166, P < 0.01; c2 = 10.4278, P < 0.01); eNOS expression in 
      colorectal carcinoma was significantly lower than in non-neoplasm colorectal 
      mucosa (c2 = 11.354, P < 0.01). The expression of iNOS correlated with 
      differentiation (c2 = 18.141, P < 0.01), invasive depth (c2 = 4.748, P < 0.01), 
      and Micro vessel density (MVD) (t = 2.327, P < 0.05). The expression of 
      HIF-1alpha was correlated with infiltrating depth (c2 = 4.397, P < 0.05), Dukeos 
      staging (c2 = 4.255, P < 0.05), and MVD (t = 2.272, P < 0.05). No correlation was 
      found in eNOS expression. CONCLUSION: Over-expression of iNOS and HIF-1alpha in 
      colorectal carcinoma is correlated with the biological character MVD.
