DP  - 2005 Nov
TI  - Overexpression of 6-phosphofructo-2-kinase/fructose-2,6-bisphosphatase-4 in the 
      human breast and colon malignant tumors.
PG  - 1005-10
AB  - 6-Phosphofructo-2-kinase/fructose-2,6-bisphosphatase (PFKFB) is a bifunctional 
      enzyme, which is responsible for maintaining the cellular level of 
      fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, a powerful allosteric activator of glycolysis. We 
      describe herein the overexpression of 
      6-phosphofructo-2-kinase/fructose-2,6-bisphosphatase-4 (PFKFB-4) isozyme in the 
      human breast and colon malignant tumors as compared to corresponding 
      non-malignant tissue counterparts. We have shown also that breast malignant cell 
      line MCF7 constitutively express PFKFB-4 mRNA and that the expression of this 
      gene is highly induced by hypoxia. Overexpression of PFKFB-4 transcript levels in 
      breast and colon malignant tumors correlates with enhanced expression of PFKFB-3, 
      hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)-1alpha and known HIF-1 dependent genes glucose 
      transporter 1 (Glut1) and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). Thus, our 
      data clearly demonstrates overexpression of PFKFB-4 mRNA and protein in the 
      breast and colon malignant tumors.
